Salmonellosis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Crampy abdominal pain, Bloody/mucus diarrhoea, High fever, History of undercooked egg/poultry ingestion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-047
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe crampy abdominal pain, high fever, and progressively bloody diarrhoea requiring urgent assessment to rule out severe dehydration or an acute surgical abdomen.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing debilitating stomach cramps, violent rigors, and multiple episodes of bloody diarrhoea preventing any oral intake. Urgent physical assessment is required to physically evaluate the abdomen for acute peritonitis and assess intravascular volume status prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is knotting up in absolute agony, I have a massive fever, and there is blood in my diarrhoea."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 36 hours ago with an abrupt onset of severe, crampy, and poorly localized periumbilical and lower abdominal pain. This was rapidly followed by a high fever, distinct severe shaking chills, and generalized systemic malaise. The patient reports diarrhoea that was initially profuse and watery but has progressed over the last 12 hours to containing macroscopic blood and distinct inflammatory mucus. Accompanied by severe nausea, recurrent forceful vomiting, and profound anorexia. The patient describes severe, excruciating generalized myalgia and intense headache. Patient reports a history of recent consumption of undercooked eggs (homemade mayonnaise) at a picnic 2 days prior.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly fatigued, ambulating to the bathroom with severe cramping pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; rapid onset of bloody diarrhoea is causing severe distress and anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 112 | Temp: 39.4 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Flushed, acutely ill-appearing, diaphoretic, curled in a fetal position holding their abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals hyperactive, high-pitched bowel sounds. There is diffuse, severe tenderness to palpation predominantly in the lower quadrants and periumbilical region. No board-like rigidity, rebound tenderness, or localized right lower quadrant peritonism. Digital rectal exam is deferred, but visual inspection confirms inflammatory mucus and macroscopic blood in the commode. Hydration assessment shows dry mucous membranes and a capillary refill of 3 seconds. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Crampy abdominal pain, Bloody/mucus diarrhoea, High fever, and History of undercooked egg/poultry ingestion.
Environmental Observations: Patient has a bucket beside the bed for vomiting.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took one dose of loperamide (advised to immediately cease).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Salmonellosis (Acute Invasive Gastroenteritis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent, massive volume of diarrhoea preceding the pain, the high fever with rigors, and the lack of focal, localized rebound tenderness at McBurney's point.

Ischemic Colitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's young age, lack of vascular risk factors, and clear infectious prodrome linked to a specific dietary exposure.

Campylobacteriosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clinically indistinguishable without a stool culture; both cause severe inflammatory bloody diarrhoea and require similar supportive management.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe dysenteric illness featuring high fever, bloody diarrhoea, and resting tachycardia indicative of moderate volume depletion. The intense, crampy abdominal pain necessitated an urgent, direct physical examination to confidently rule out a life-threatening acute surgical abdomen (such as appendicitis or perforation). Delaying this assessment, or allowing the continued use of contraindicated anti-motility agents (loperamide), carried a high risk of toxic megacolon or severe hypovolaemic shock. This presentation strictly fulfills the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advise immediate cessation of all anti-motility agents (Loperamide/Imodium). Prescribe an oral antiemetic (Ondansetron) to facilitate oral rehydration. Prescribe oral rehydration salts (ORS). Antibiotics are generally withheld in healthy immunocompetent hosts to prevent prolonged carriage, but arrange for a stool multiplex PCR and culture tomorrow to confirm the pathogen.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the antiemetic precisely 30 minutes before attempting to drink the ORS, the patient will successfully suppress the gag reflex; keeping the electrolyte fluid down will reward them with an immediate reduction in severe muscle cramping and lightheadedness, reinforcing the strict hydration protocol.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48-72 hours to review stool culture results and monitor symptom resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient develops severe, rigid, and constant (non-cramping) abdominal pain, completely stops passing urine for 8 hours, becomes profoundly confused, or experiences a massive amount of frank red blood from the rectum.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically presumed to be an invasive bacterial enteritis; stool culture is required for definitive identification.
Clinical Course: The extreme fever and vomiting usually peak within 48 hours, while the crampy bloody diarrhoea may take 4 to 7 days to fully resolve in a healthy adult.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The bacteria have severely inflamed the lining of your bowel, causing the blood and severe cramps. If the pain suddenly changes from waves of cramps to a constant, agonizing, sharp pain that makes it impossible to move, you must go to the emergency department immediately to rule out a perforated bowel.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have severe food poisoning from the undercooked eggs. We do not usually give antibiotics for this because they can actually trap the bacteria in your gut for longer. Your body needs to physically flush the infection out. You must stop taking anti-diarrhoea medication, as it paralyzes your bowel and keeps the bacteria inside you.
Actionable Steps: Throw away the Loperamide/Imodium. Mix the ORS exactly as directed. Wash your hands meticulously with soap and water after every bathroom trip to avoid infecting others.

SMS INFORMATION

Presumed Salmonella (food poisoning). Stop all anti-diarrhoea tablets immediately. Start anti-nausea medication and sip rehydration fluids constantly.

SMS EDUCATION

Take anti-nausea meds 30 mins before drinking. Wash hands thoroughly. Go to ED immediately if you stop passing urine or if stomach pain becomes constant and agonizing.
